{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive suppression",
    "counter strategy",
    "pick and roll failure",
    "pull-up jumper",
    "points per possession lift",
    "league-wide analysis",
    "post-failure adaptation"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "defining PNR failure",
    "identifying pull-up as counter after PNR fail",
    "measuring lift in points per possession",
    "choice of unit of analysis for league-wide test",
    "sample size and power for threshold 70%",
    "temporal ordering and leakage",
    "regression-to-the-mean after failure conditioning"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF13",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Within-game did_adapt toward SHOULD counters improves PPP, but the apparent adapter advantage (+0.05–0.07 PPP) was produced by selection and regression-to-the-mean artifacts, not a genuine counter lift."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Play-type classifier assisted-flag leak: SPOT_UP 2.83 PPP (100% makes) because the assisted flag existed only on made shots. After rewrite, SPOT_UP ~1.16 PPP. Shows that outcome-derived labels can catastrophically inflate play-type efficiency."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Turnover-force matchup decomposition on four playoff games: player-channel n often 15–28 defensive possessions; primary defender attribution includes helper steals. Failure: exposure sparsity makes reliable lift estimation impossible."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "On-ball defensive scheme labeling: only 14.9% and 3.3% of possessions labeled in two teams; 'switches_on' field unpopulated. Label error and small sample prevented any usable scheme-lift analysis."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-004",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Within-series DID-ADAPT case study (NYK vs PHI): n=8 team-game rows; series SHOULD vector missing; adaptation effects untestable due to small sample."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-063",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Holdout play-type rank validation: median rank correlation +0.539, but top-3 lift partly reflects league-wide high-efficiency play types (CUT, PUTBACK, TRANSITION); 10.5% of rows misleading (rho<0). Highlights that apparent counter lift can be an artefact of play-type base rates."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF13",
      "what_failed": "Within-game adaptation lift via SHOULD counters",
      "why": "Selection and regression-to-the-mean artefacts; adapter dPPP advantage vanished after controlling for early PPP quintiles; SWITCH subgroup reversed."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "what_failed": "Possession-level play-type PPP from original PBP classifier",
      "why": "Assisted-flag leakage (flag present only on makes) produced absurd PPP values (SPOT_UP 2.83). Demonstrates how easily outcome-dependent labelling contaminates efficiency measures."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "what_failed": "Matchup-level decomposition of turnover forcing",
      "why": "Exposure sparsity: player-channel n often 15–28, too small for reliable PPP estimation; primary_defender attribution includes helper steals, further blurring the channel."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "what_failed": "Labelling on-ball defensive schemes from NBA matchup/PBP fields",
      "why": "Label rates 3–15%; 'switches_on' unpopulated; classification error and small sample made scheme-based analysis untestable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-004",
      "what_failed": "Within-series DID-ADAPT analysis",
      "why": "Sample size n=8 team-game rows; missing SHOULD vector prevented computation; small sample made adaptation effects untestable."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Conditional lift analyses that condition on a failure event are highly vulnerable to regression-to-the-mean (SF13).",
    "Play-type PPP measures derived from play-by-play can be contaminated by outcome-derived flags, producing catastrophic overestimates (SF-PAP-096).",
    "Rare play sequences yield very small cell sizes, making reliable PPP estimates impossible at the level of individual teams or matchups (SF-APPA-066, SF-APPA-004).",
    "Even play-type ranking models show that top-performing play types often simply reflect league-wide high-efficiency play types rather than genuine counter effects (SF-APPA-063).",
    "Defensive scheme labels from standard NBA data feeds are too sparse and error-prone to condition a counter analysis (SF-APPS-058)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether PULL_UP after a PNR failure yields positive lift.",
    "The magnitude or prevalence of any such lift across NBA teams or seasons.",
    "That the 'PNR fail → PULL_UP' sequence occurs frequently enough to support a league-wide test.",
    "That any observed lift would be causal rather than an artefact of selection, base rates, or data leakage."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The study would require defining 'PNR fail' precisely (e.g., turnover, missed shot, defensive stop with zero points) and identifying the next possession's play type as PULL_UP. The combined filter will produce very small sample sizes per team-season, likely far below what is needed to estimate PPP lift with adequate power.",
    "The threshold of '>=70% of units with median lift>2pp' implies a per-unit significance test; with ~30 teams, even if the effect is real, sampling variation and multiple testing will make the criterion very difficult to meet without large samples.",
    "The absence of a pre-specified counterfactual baseline (lift relative to what?) makes the hypothesis ill-posed; inadvertent choice of baseline could create or mask an effect.",
    "Even if a positive lift were observed, it would be indistinguishable from regression to the mean following a failure event, or from the generally high efficiency of certain shot types (like PULL_UP) relative to league average."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Exact definition of 'PNR fail' not given by the research question; what counts as a failure (e.g., any non-score, only turnovers, shot clock violations) is ambiguous.",
    "Baseline PPP for computing 'lift' is unspecified: league-average PULL_UP PPP, team-average PULL_UP PPP, or PPP after non-failure PNRs?",
    "The unit of analysis ('units') is not stated; typical candidates are team-season or team-series, each with different power implications.",
    "The number of possessions meeting the PNR-fail-then-PULL_UP sequence has not been estimated; without a pilot count, sample size is unknown but likely small.",
    "Temporal constraints: 2025-26 season is partial; using full dataset may still yield sparse sequences."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without historical evidence, one might optimistically attempt a simple PPP comparison. The prior work shows that conditional lift after a failure (SF13) was an RTM artefact, play-type PPP can be wildly distorted by outcome leaks (SF-PAP-096), and rare-event sample sizes prevent reliable estimation (SF-APPA-066, SF-APPA-004). These findings collectively demonstrate that a naive test of this hypothesis is almost certain to produce a misleading result, shifting the decision from potential proceed to do_not_proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.05,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "PNR-fail-then-PULL_UP sequences are rare per team-season; expected counts likely too low to estimate per-unit PPP lift with sufficient precision for the 70% / 2pp thresholds."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Even league-wide, the number of such sequences may be sparse, making median lift estimates unstable and power for per-unit tests very low."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Conditioning on a PNR failure selects low-PPP possessions; subsequent PULL_UP efficiency will regress upward even if pull-up is not a counter, creating a spurious lift."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF13",
      "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-063"
    ],
    "summary": "No prior study tested the exact hypothesis that PULL_UP after PNR failure is a league-wide counter. However, earlier work on adaptation lift after defensive failures (SF13) was found to be an RTM artefact; play-type efficiency measures were contaminated by look-ahead leaks (SF-PAP-096); and rare-event decomposition suffered from extreme exposure sparsity (SF-APPA-066). These findings are directly applicable to the proposed analysis and strongly caution against a simple PPP-comparison test."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Conditioning on PNR failure selects possessions with low outcomes; any subsequent play type will appear to have higher PPP due to regression to the mean, even without a counter effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Play-type classification uses assisted flags that may exist only for made shots; if unaddressed, pull-up PPP could be inflated by makes that were assisted, mimicking a lift."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Identifying PNR and PULL_UP from PBP data relies on accurate play-type inference; classification errors (as seen in SF-PAP-096, SF-APPS-058) can bias PPP estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "PNR-fail-then-PULL_UP sequence is expected to be rare; per-unit sample sizes likely too small for reliable lift estimation (analogous to SF-APPA-066)."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Even if the effect exists, the test demands that >=70% of units show positive lift with median >2pp; with few sequences per unit, power to detect such an effect is extremely low."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "PULL_UP jumpers after a PNR failure may systematically differ in shot quality (defender proximity, shot clock, score differential) from pull-ups in other contexts, confounding the lift estimate."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing positive lift across many units inflates false positive risk; without appropriate multiplicity correction, the 70%-of-units threshold may be trivially met by chance."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If PNR failure is defined using the outcome of the possession (e.g., missed shot), the definition itself conditions on the very outcome we later analyse, creating circularity."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.02,
    "rejected": 0.03,
    "untestable": 0.45,
    "artifact": 0.40,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect on points per possession (PPP) of a team executing a PULL_UP jump shot on the possession immediately following a pick-and-roll (PNR) that ended without points (turnover, missed field goal, non-shooting foul, or shot-clock reset), relative to that team's pull-up PPP in comparable game states that do not follow a failed PNR, adjusting for shot quality and context.",
    "unit": "Team-season (up to 120 units across four seasons, though 2025-26 is partial at time of query)",
    "sample": "All NBA possessions from 2022-23 through 2025-26 (complete regular season plus playoffs) where a PNR is identifiable and results in a failure, and the next possession by the same team is a PULL_UP field-goal attempt. A pilot run must estimate the expected count per team-season; if median < 25, the test is underpowered.",
    "power": "Assuming a true median lift of 2.5 pp (0.025 PPP) with SD ~0.15 PPP per unit, a one-sample t-test per unit at alpha=0.05 would require roughly 35-40 observations per unit for 80% power. Even 25 observations per unit would leave most units underpowered. The 70% threshold further reduces effective power. Formal power simulation needed.",
    "controls": "Shot distance, defender proximity (from tracking data if available), shot-clock remaining, period, score margin at start of possession, team fixed effects, season fixed effects. Baseline PPP estimated from team's pull-up shots in the same game state but not preceded by a PNR failure, using linear mixed models.",
    "falsification": "1) Repeat the analysis for a placebo play type (e.g., CATCH_SHOOT) that should not be a counter; lift should be absent. 2) Repeat the analysis for pull-up after a successful PNR (score); the lift should be smaller or absent. 3) Check for regression-to-the-mean: compare PPP on the preceding PNR-fail possession with the team's overall PNR PPP; if the fail is unusually low, a subsequent lift may simply be mean reversion. 4) Use a pre-registered non-parametric bootstrap of team-season labels to assess chance of observing 70% positive under a global null.",
    "multiplicity": "Use Benjamini-Hochberg FDR control across all team-season units for the per-unit test of positive lift. Additionally, test the global hypothesis that median lift >2pp using a one-sample Wilcoxon test on the unit-level lifts.",
    "decision_rule": "Consider the hypothesis supported only if (a) at least 70% of team-season units show positive lift with FDR-adjusted one-sided p < 0.05, (b) the median lift across all units exceeds 0.02 PPP, and (c) all falsification checks pass at nominal alpha 0.05.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 per-game PBP and derived play-type possession records, with the post-rewrite play-type classifier that does not use assisted flags for unrealised outcomes."
  }
}}

